WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
The Bay of Fundy in eastern Canada
has the highest tides in the world,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.560
anything up to 16 metres difference
between low tide and high tide.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.780
And the effects of that show up
in a few places around here.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.840
Like Hopewell Rocks, where I'm not
standing on a beach right now,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.460
I'm standing on the ocean floor.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.900
That's how far the tide is out.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.760
Actually, I'm kind of
sinking into it.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.020
But in a few hours' time,

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.880
those rocks will be
half-way underwater,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.720
and if I stuck around,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.800
I would be drowning in very cold,
very fast and very deep ocean.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.720
But I'm not going to do that!

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.480
Instead, I'm going to go about
40km that way, to--

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.060
--Moncton. It's the next morning,
it's very cold,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.660
and I'm flying a drone to try and
get a shot of the tidal bore.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.720
This is one of the few places in
the world where, every high tide,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.080
a wave of water washes upstream
against the current.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.680
But the most obvious sign of the tides
around here is in--

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.600
--Saint John, at the Reversing Falls,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.360
where a massive river
meets the Bay of Fundy

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.940
at a narrow channel
filled with rocks and snags.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.480
This isn't just a tidal river:
it's a tidal set of rapids.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.820
Calling it a "waterfall"
is maybe a bit generous,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.460
but at low tide the water
definitely has a gradient,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.860
it drops a couple of metres in height
over a really short distance.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.720
And then a few hours later at--

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.240
--high tide, the whole
flow reverses,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.880
and the river's dropping a few
metres in height the other way.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.280
And so the obvious question I had was:

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.880
why has nobody turned that bay
into a power station yet?

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.500
Actually, question number one:
why are the tides so high here?

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.800
To answer that, I'll use the
bathtub in my hotel room.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.320
For this "scientifically-rigorous"
demonstration,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.260
the Bay of Fundy is represented
by the bathtub,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.080
and the Atlantic tides and all
the unimaginable pressure

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.200
created by megatonnes of water
is represented by...

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.940
...my arm. So.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.180
Moon pulls on the ocean, tide
comes in, pressure gets applied,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.920
and a wave heads down the bathtub,
the Bay of Fundy, and bounces back.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.320
'Cos it's not like the water
all rises simultaneously.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.680
It takes time, and yes, the scale
here is colossally different,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.240
but you still get a wave effect,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.280
a reflection, and a trough
that follows the wave.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.720
Tick, tock. Tick, tock.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.120
And that will always take
roughly the same time

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.080
to go up and bounce back,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.180
it'll be affected a little bit by
the weather and water temperature.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.960
So what happens when the
next tide comes in?

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.400
If I time each push to go with
the wave that's already there,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.300
then very quickly, you start seeing
a resonant frequency,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.100
and a lot of power...

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.450
Wah!

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.320
That worked way better
than I expected it to!

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.640
The resonant frequency of the
Bay of Fundy is roughly 12½ hours,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.820
the same as the tidal cycle.
Combine that with geography

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.620
that's less like a bathtub
and more like a funnel,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.600
getting tighter and tighter
as that wave comes in,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.140
and you have the highest tides
in the world, right there.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.140
The power is immense
and it's obvious.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.380
And if it could be harnessed,
then it'd be reliable and renewable.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.300
Intermittent, sure,
but on a schedule

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.940
that would be simple for the grid
to predict and plan around.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.200
So why aren't there tidal power stations
all around the Bay of Fundy?

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.120
Well, the answer is: it's been tried.
A lot. Usually in the--

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.980
--Minas Basin, where several companies

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.980
have put millions and millions of dollars
behind technical trials.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.700
And every time, the Bay of Fundy
breaks the turbines!

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.220
2009: a turbine was "torn apart".

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.260
2010: two blades "broken off".

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.540
2018: "damaged beyond repair".

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.300
Turbines in these waters have
to be light enough to spin,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.700
but strong enough to resist
high speed salt water

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.460
that carries huge amounts of sediment
and big rocks along with it,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.440
and in winter, enormous
chunks of sea ice.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.380
Oh, and that's before we even
start talking about the concerns

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.960
from First Nations peoples
and commercial fishing operations

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.140
that maybe putting a load of
giant spinning things in the water

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.060
might cause some problems for fish.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.440
But people are still trying, there are
more experimental turbines planned.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.340
Can it be done?

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:42.660
Maybe.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.880
I thought about doing long interviews

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.280
with the tidal power companies
and their opponents around here,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.880
but those interviews would have
sounded the same twenty years ago.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.000
And the result would have just been
a much longer way of saying:

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.540
maybe.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.820
If it can ever be done,
safely and reliably,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.320
it'd be brilliant,
I hope that happens.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.140
But there's also a decent chance
that the Bay of Fundy

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.880
is just going to tear the
turbines apart again,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.360
and in twenty years, the answer
still will be: maybe.

